# Project Summary---- 'Credit Analysis'

This project analyses 1.6 million Lending Club unsecured personal loans (2007–2018, ~$42B in originations) using pure Python to uncover 
portfolio-level credit risk patterns and produce bank-quality recommendations.
No machine learning in the core analysis — the work uses the frameworks credit analysts actually use: vintage curves, 
loss decomposition, risk-return pricing, hypothesis testing, and macro-credit linkage.

The central finding is structural mispricing. Grades E, F, and G carry negative interest spreads of -3.4%, -6.7%, and -5.9% —
these grades lose money before funding costs.Recovery rates are negligible across the board (below 3.7% of originations), and
after accounting for early payoff, effective yields are only 37–63% of stated rates.The worst-performing vintages (2012Q2 at 
17.7% lifetime loss) came from the growth phase, not the financial crisis.
Five hypothesis tests confirmed key risk factors: renters default 3.56pp more than mortgage holders, high-unemployment quarters add 3.98pp 
to charge-off rates, post-2012 rates are structurally ~6pp higher, DTI shows non-linear risk acceleration above 20%, and Grade G default variance is 7.4× that of Grade A.

OLS regression on macro data produced a negative coefficient — explained by the vintage lag effect — with the
corrected stress test estimating $856M in additional losses under a +2pp unemployment shock.

**ML Extensions:** A WOE/IV logistic scorecard (AUC 0.695, Gini 0.390) was built using industry-standard PDO scoring. 
XGBoost as a challenger model achieved AUC 0.696 — a 0.001 difference — confirming the credit signal is overwhelmingly 
linear and the transparent scorecard is the right choice. SHAP analysis showed interest rate dominates all other features combined.
K-Means segmentation (k=5) identified five borrower segments ranging from 4% to 23% default rate.

